1
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:35,630
Reaction Workup 1. Extracting, washing, and drying.

2
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:40,350
As all chemists quickly learn, it is very rare for a chemical reaction

3
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:43,950
to provide only the one compound that you're looking for.

4
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:49,150
Even highly selective reactions generate the desired product as a crude mixture

5
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:54,350
containing byproducts and unreacted starting materials and reagents.

6
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:57,630
Liquid-liquid extraction is the most common technique

7
00:00:57,630 --> 00:01:01,550
for separating a compound from a complex mixture.

8
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:05,950
In an extraction, you can take advantage of differing solubilities

9
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:09,550
to selectively separate the different components.

10
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:13,390
By carefully planning out an extraction and washing sequence,

11
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:18,430
otherwise known as a workup, the desired product can usually be

12
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:23,390
separated from the unwanted impurities. This video will illustrate the proper

13
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:28,860
technique involved in working up a reaction.

14
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:32,940
In an ideal world, each chemical reaction in the laboratory

15
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:37,020
would provide only the desired product in its pure form.

16
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:41,820
In reality, the product is almost always generated as part of a messy mixture of

17
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:46,140
byproducts and sideproducts. In short, garbage.

18
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:49,180
Liquid-liquid extraction is a useful technique

19
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:53,580
used to isolate the desired compound from a complex mixture.

20
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:57,340
In an extraction, the mixture is partitioned between two

21
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:00,220
immiscible solvents in a separatory funnel,

22
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:05,500
otherwise known as a SEP funnel. The key is that the two solvents are mutually

23
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:09,420
insoluble, so they form two distinct layers.

24
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:13,100
One of the solvents is almost always water, and the other

25
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:18,620
is an organic solvent of your choice. The different components of the mixture

26
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:22,220
are selectively distributed in one solvent or the other,

27
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,900
depending on their different solubilities. In this way,

28
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:30,220
you can take advantage of differing solubilities to selectively transport

29
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:37,520
solutes from one layer to another. In most cases, but not all,

30
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,360
neutral organic molecules prefer the organic phase,

31
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:46,800
while charged molecules and inorganic salts prefer the aqueous layer.

32
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,560
By carefully planning out an extraction and washing sequence,

33
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:55,360
otherwise known as a reaction workup, the desired compound can usually be

34
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:58,640
separated from most of the unwanted impurities.

35
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:03,120
This brings up an important point. What is the difference between an extraction

36
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:07,680
and a wash? These terms describe similar but fundamentally different

37
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:14,240
operations. An extraction involves pulling the desired compound out of a mixture of

38
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:18,560
compounds, whereas in a wash, you are pulling

39
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:22,400
unwanted impurities away from the desired product.

40
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,920
In other words, you always keep the extracts

41
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:29,600
and trash the washes. This might sound confusing,

42
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:33,600
but it will hopefully become clear as we continue.

43
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,960
The first step in an extraction and washing sequence,

44
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:40,240
once you have decided on the appropriate solvents, of course,

45
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:44,960
is filling the sep funnel. It is a good idea to use an iron ring

46
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:49,520
and a nice cushy cork ring to safely support the sep funnel.

47
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:54,720
Before you add any solvent, close the stopcock

48
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:59,040
and place a clean dry flask under the sep funnel.

49
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:02,640
This will prevent you from dumping precious material all over the dirty

50
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:06,640
bench. The flask will save you if the funnel

51
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:11,200
leaks or the stopcock somehow opened itself.

52
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,280
When the stopcock is safely closed and the flask is in place,

53
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:18,880
pour your two solvents into the sep funnel.

54
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:22,640
Use a sep funnel large enough that you don't fill it more than three quarters

55
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:25,920
full. If it is too full, you won't be able to

56
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:31,820
efficiently mix the solvents. This brings us to the next step,

57
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:37,020
mixing and venting. It is important to mix the two solvents very well.

58
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:41,900
This increases the contact surface area and allows the distribution of solutes to

59
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:46,860
occur very rapidly. But use caution,

60
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:52,700
mixing will frequently cause pressure to build up inside of the sep funnel.

61
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:56,940
Pressure buildup is especially common when you are using particularly

62
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:01,180
volatile solvents like diethyl ether

63
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:07,630
or compounds that can generate gas like sodium bicarbonate.

64
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:11,390
Under acidic conditions, sodium bicarbonate will release

65
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:17,300
gaseous carbon dioxide. Before any vigorous mixing,

66
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:22,260
it is a good idea to gently swirl and invert the sep funnel.

67
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:27,300
Then invert the sep funnel, holding the stopper tightly.

68
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:31,220
Allow the liquid to drain away from the stopcock

69
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:34,500
and point the tip of the sep funnel away from yourself

70
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:41,540
and other people. Slowly open the stopcock to allow venting.

71
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:45,700
Now you're ready to really mix it up. Hold the sep funnel firmly

72
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:48,740
and shake it vigorously for several seconds.

73
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:52,980
Once again, invert the funnel and vent it carefully.

74
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:56,980
Repeat the shaking and venting until you no longer hear gas escape

75
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:01,060
when you open the stopcock. Then set your funnel down

76
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:09,280
and let the layers settle. Ideally, two layers with a nice clean

77
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:17,760
interface should form very quickly. Unfortunately, things don't always go

78
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:24,370
so smoothly. A thick cloudy layer called an emulsion

79
00:06:24,370 --> 00:06:27,810
can sometimes form between the two solvent layers.

80
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:31,490
An emulsion is a colloidal mixture of the two solvents

81
00:06:31,490 --> 00:06:36,610
and is frequently caused by the presence of fine particles in the solution.

82
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:40,370
Getting rid of an emulsion can take some time, so if you know that you are

83
00:06:40,370 --> 00:06:43,970
likely to generate one, your best bet is to take pains to

84
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:48,530
prevent it ahead of time. For example, gently swirling and

85
00:06:48,530 --> 00:06:53,090
inverting your sep funnel will rarely cause an emulsion to form.

86
00:06:53,090 --> 00:06:57,170
You will have to spend more time mixing because swirling is not as efficient as

87
00:06:57,170 --> 00:07:01,090
shaking, but it may be worth it. Don't worry,

88
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:04,130
if you unwittingly generated a monster emulsion,

89
00:07:04,130 --> 00:07:09,330
you still have a few options. First, you can just sit and wait. It might

90
00:07:09,330 --> 00:07:13,570
clear itself up, even if it takes a few hours. If you

91
00:07:13,570 --> 00:07:16,210
don't have all day to finish your extraction,

92
00:07:16,210 --> 00:07:22,290
try swirling the mixture gently and stirring the emulsion with a glass rod.

93
00:07:22,290 --> 00:07:26,690
If this doesn't work, add several milliliters of saturated sodium chloride

94
00:07:26,690 --> 00:07:31,650
solution to the funnel and swirl to mix. The sodium chloride

95
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:34,850
increases the ionic strength of the aqueous layer

96
00:07:34,850 --> 00:07:38,130
and decreases the solubility of the organic solvent

97
00:07:38,130 --> 00:07:43,490
in the water. As a last resort, you may need to use vacuum filtration

98
00:07:43,490 --> 00:07:48,210
to filter your entire mixture through a pad of sea light.

99
00:07:48,210 --> 00:07:51,650
When you are carrying out an extraction and washing sequence,

100
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:55,730
it is very important that you keep track of which layer is which

101
00:07:55,730 --> 00:07:59,090
and what compounds are dissolved in each layer.

102
00:07:59,090 --> 00:08:02,530
This means that you constantly need to be asking yourself,

103
00:08:02,530 --> 00:08:07,330
which layer is on the top? Which layer is on the bottom?

104
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:11,090
One way to keep track of this is to know something about the densities

105
00:08:11,170 --> 00:08:14,690
of the solvents you are using. In every case,

106
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:17,970
the solvent with the lowest density will be on top,

107
00:08:17,970 --> 00:08:22,370
and the solvent with the highest density will be on the bottom.

108
00:08:22,370 --> 00:08:25,330
This is helpful because you can generally assume

109
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:30,690
that dilute aqueous solutions have a density around one gram per milliliter,

110
00:08:30,690 --> 00:08:36,370
similar to water. The organic solvent will be in the top layer

111
00:08:36,370 --> 00:08:39,890
if it has a density less than one gram per milliliter,

112
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:45,570
such as hexane, diethyl ether, or ethyl acetate.

113
00:08:45,570 --> 00:08:49,890
The organic solvent will be the bottom layer if it has a density greater than

114
00:08:49,890 --> 00:08:53,330
one gram per milliliter, such as methylene chloride or

115
00:08:53,330 --> 00:08:58,800
chloroform. But be careful. High concentrations of

116
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:03,440
solutes can sometimes drastically affect the density of a solvent.

117
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:06,720
If you're having trouble figuring out which layer is which,

118
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:13,180
you may need to perform a quick solubility test.

119
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:16,220
Take a couple of drops of the layer in question

120
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:22,860
and add them to a small amount of water in a test tube.

121
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:26,300
If the drops dissolve without turning the water cloudy,

122
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:34,580
then the drops were from the aqueous layer. If the solution turns cloudy,

123
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:38,100
or the drops form an insoluble layer on the bottom,

124
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:46,620
or an insoluble layer on the top, then the drops are from the organic layer.

125
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:49,660
But even if you think you know which layer is which,

126
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:53,500
do not discard any of the layers until you are absolutely sure

127
00:09:53,500 --> 00:10:00,130
that you have isolated all of the material. Once you have a good idea of

128
00:10:00,130 --> 00:10:04,210
where your material is, it's time to separate the layers.

129
00:10:04,210 --> 00:10:08,690
Before you do anything, place a clean dry labeled beaker

130
00:10:08,690 --> 00:10:15,140
under the step funnel and remove the stopper.

131
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:22,000
If you don't do this, you'll have some trouble getting any liquid to drain.

132
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,640
With the stopper off, open the stopcock and allow the bottom layer to drain

133
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:33,250
into the beaker. Take it slow when you get near the interface

134
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:42,880
so that you can close the valve precisely in between the two layers.

135
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:46,880
Once you have drained the bottom layer, you have a few options.

136
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:51,120
If you still need to perform a wash or an extraction with the top layer,

137
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:54,640
then leave it in the funnel. Pour in the second solvent

138
00:10:54,640 --> 00:11:00,560
and proceed to mix and vent. If you are done with the top layer,

139
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,680
then pick up the funnel and pour it into a second

140
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:11,010
clean dry labeled beaker. Here is a little bit of step funnel etiquette to

141
00:11:11,010 --> 00:11:14,050
follow. When you are separating the layers,

142
00:11:14,050 --> 00:11:17,010
always drain the bottom layer through the stopcock

143
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:20,770
and pour the top layer through the top. This will minimize

144
00:11:20,770 --> 00:11:27,870
recontamination of your material. Before we move on to proper post

145
00:11:27,870 --> 00:11:31,550
extraction procedures, let's walk through a sample reaction

146
00:11:31,550 --> 00:11:34,030
workup similar to one you might use in the

147
00:11:34,030 --> 00:11:37,070
laboratory. Let's say that you performed an

148
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:43,070
acylation reaction to generate phenylacetate. At the end of the reaction,

149
00:11:43,070 --> 00:11:46,830
you were left with a solution containing a lot more than the material you were

150
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:50,910
looking for. So what do you do? First, you know

151
00:11:50,910 --> 00:11:56,030
that phenylacetate is soluble in diethyl ether and that diethyl ether

152
00:11:56,030 --> 00:12:00,910
is insoluble in water. Therefore, in your workup, you will use

153
00:12:00,910 --> 00:12:05,550
diethyl ether as the organic layer and wash it with different aqueous

154
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:11,630
solutions to remove the impurities. Now it is time to plan out the washes.

155
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:15,230
It is important that you use what you know about the impurities

156
00:12:15,230 --> 00:12:19,550
to decide on appropriate aqueous washes for your mixture.

157
00:12:19,550 --> 00:12:23,470
Keep in mind that the washes should only be 10 to 50 percent

158
00:12:23,470 --> 00:12:26,750
of the volume of the solution that you are washing.

159
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:30,270
And make sure to repeat each wash two or three times

160
00:12:30,270 --> 00:12:35,940
to wash away as much of the impurities as possible.

161
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:42,900
Your crude mixture contains acetic acid, a fairly strong acid, and two weak bases

162
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:49,120
triethylamine and demap. To get rid of the acetic acid,

163
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:53,840
you need to wash the solution with a mild base that will deprotonate the acid

164
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:58,320
and pull the charged acetate ion into the aqueous layer.

165
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:02,880
Saturated sodium bicarbonate works very well for this purpose.

166
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:06,640
Add the sodium bicarbonate solution and mix well.

167
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:10,720
Make sure that you vent frequently to release the carbon dioxide gas that is

168
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:14,480
generated. Finally, drain away the aqueous

169
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:17,680
solution that now contains the sodium acetate

170
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:25,790
and repeat the wash two more times. To get rid of the weak bases triethylamine

171
00:13:25,790 --> 00:13:28,670
and demap, you need to wash with a dilute

172
00:13:28,670 --> 00:13:32,910
solution of a strong acid that will protonate the bases and pull

173
00:13:32,910 --> 00:13:38,430
them into the aqueous layer. A 10% aqueous solution of HCl works

174
00:13:38,430 --> 00:13:43,070
well for this. Once again, add the wash and mix and vent

175
00:13:43,070 --> 00:13:47,230
well. Drain the aqueous layer containing the

176
00:13:47,230 --> 00:13:54,060
protonated amines and repeat two more times.

177
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:57,660
So far, this looks like a pretty effective reaction workup,

178
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:00,780
but there is one more thing to keep in mind.

179
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:04,700
Water is slightly soluble in ether, not to a large extent,

180
00:14:04,700 --> 00:14:08,780
but there is some water hanging around in the organic layer.

181
00:14:08,780 --> 00:14:13,180
One way to pull some of the water out is to wash it with saturated sodium

182
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:17,420
chloride, otherwise known as brine. The high ionic

183
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:21,340
strength of the salt solution decreases the solubility of the

184
00:14:21,340 --> 00:14:25,500
aqueous layer in the ether, effectively washing away some of the

185
00:14:25,500 --> 00:14:31,840
water. Voila! Now that you have done all you

186
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:35,680
can with your sep funnel, it's time to dry your organic layer even

187
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:43,140
more with a drying agent. Magnesium sulfate is one popular drying agent

188
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:48,100
because it is quick and effective. It is also a very fine powder,

189
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:51,620
so take care that you don't leave any of your desired material

190
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:55,060
absorbed onto the surface when you discard it.

191
00:14:55,060 --> 00:14:59,060
Keep in mind that magnesium sulfate is also slightly acidic,

192
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:05,630
so it may not be suitable for use with compounds that are sensitive to acid.

193
00:15:05,630 --> 00:15:08,990
To dry the organic layer with magnesium sulfate,

194
00:15:08,990 --> 00:15:12,590
add a small amount of the powder and swirl.

195
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:16,030
Incrementally add more drying agent and swirl

196
00:15:16,030 --> 00:15:21,340
until you get the snow globe effect. There should be free,

197
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:29,440
unclumped powder in the flask, even after letting it sit for a few minutes.

198
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:32,880
Sodium sulfate is another common drying agent.

199
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:37,680
Although it is slower than magnesium sulfate, it is effectively neutral

200
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:42,000
and in its granular form will absorb less of your desired material

201
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,920
onto its surface. Drying the organic layer with sodium sulfate

202
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:52,320
is similar to using magnesium sulfate, except you may need to look a bit

203
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:57,680
closer to see whether there is free, unclumped drying agent in the flask.

204
00:15:57,680 --> 00:16:01,680
It is also a good idea to let the solution dry for five or ten minutes

205
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:07,200
before removing the drying agent. To remove the drying agent,

206
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:11,440
perform a gravity filtration with fluted filter paper.

207
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:14,400
Once you have filtered the entire solution,

208
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:17,440
be sure to rinse the drying agent very well

209
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:21,360
so that you don't leave any product stuck to the surface.

210
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,800
It is a good idea to rinse the drying agent three times

211
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:32,380
with clean dry solvent, adding each of these rinses to the flask.

212
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:36,060
Once you are confident that you have transferred all of the desired material

213
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:40,140
to the round bottom flask, you are ready to proceed to the Rotavap

214
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:45,580
and concentrate the solution in Vacuo. For detailed information on proper use of

215
00:16:45,580 --> 00:16:48,300
the Rotavap, you can watch the second reaction

216
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:56,510
workup video. Remember, this video is intended to help

217
00:16:56,510 --> 00:17:00,190
you prepare for lab by providing a demonstration of the proper

218
00:17:00,190 --> 00:17:04,110
experimental technique. It is not intended as a replacement

219
00:17:04,110 --> 00:17:08,190
for reading your lab manual or the supplementary material.

220
00:17:08,190 --> 00:17:11,630
In order to become a great experimentalist, it is important that you

221
00:17:11,630 --> 00:17:18,510
understand both theory and technique. Now it's your turn. Good luck!
